tags.description:
Clients of mailcow's own OAuth2 provider. Applications use the unchanged legacy endpoints on
the same host:

- authorization endpoint `/oauth/authorize`, token endpoint `/oauth/token`, resource page
  `/oauth/profile` (user details of the signed-in mailbox);
- grant type "Authorization Code"; refresh tokens are issued, and a new refresh token replaces
  a used one;
- the default and only scope is `profile`, which also applies when the scope parameter is
  omitted; only mailbox users can authorise applications, on the legacy login page;
- the `state` parameter is required in the authorize request;
- access tokens are valid for 24 hours and refresh tokens for 31 days (mailcow's
  `ACCESS_TOKEN_LIFETIME` and `REFRESH_TOKEN_LIFETIME`);
- when a client has several redirect URIs, the authorize request must name one;
- regenerating the client secret does not expire existing authorization codes or tokens, but
  renewing a token with the old secret fails; revoking the tokens ends every application
  session at once.

delete.description:
Deletes the alias domain together with:
- its entries in mailcow's domain map and rate limits;
- the aliases that live in it, and the explicit sender permissions to send as their addresses;
- the explicit sender permissions for the alias domain as a whole;
- the temporary aliases of its addresses;
- its domain-level BCC maps (local destination `@<alias domain>` or the bare name);
- its spam allow and block lists;
- its name in the target domain's footer exclusions.

The DKIM key is kept, as for domains, so an alias domain or domain created later with the same
name reuses it. The groupware directory is updated. Everything is removed together or not at
all; when the shared store is unavailable the request answers `503` and removes nothing. This
is the same cleanup that domain deletion (feature 002) applies to the alias domains of a
deleted domain. Audited with what else was removed.

post.description:
Deletes up to 100 mailbox templates in one request, as the legacy mass deletion does.
Administrators only, from a session or a read-write key; any other caller is refused as a whole
(`403`). More than 100 targets are refused as a whole (`422`, `tooMany` on `targets`, with
`max: 100`).

Otherwise each distinct template is processed like the single deletion, with its checks and
audit record (which carries the bulk request ID), and gets one result in request order:
- `done`: deleted;
- `notFound`: no mailbox template has this ID;
- `refused`: the Default template (`mailbox-template-protected`);
- `outdated`: the target's `version` no longer matches (`412` problem); the template is kept;
- `failed`: an unexpected failure.

Targets without `version` are not version-checked. Mailboxes created from a deleted template
keep their values.

SyncJobBulkTarget.description:
One sync job of a bulk action. `id` is the job's `id` written as a decimal string, as in its
path. `version` is the job's `etag` as last read (the surrounding double quotes may be left out);
an outdated tag gives the target `outdated`. Version tags of `reschedule` targets are not checked.

BulkResult.description:
Result of a bulk action. Each target is authorised, validated, processed and audited on its own;
one failure does not stop the others. Every audit record of the request carries
`bulkRequestId`.

IdentityProviderGenericOidcInput.description:
Whole generic OIDC configuration. `clientSecret` is required when no generic OIDC configuration
is stored and when `tokenEndpoint` differs from the stored one (rule code `secretRequired`);
otherwise an omitted secret keeps the stored one.

ContainerProblem.description:
Problem about one container: `container-protected`, `container-not-running`,
`container-action-failed` and `mail-service-not-running`. `container` names the container;
`serviceMessage` carries the container service's message for `container-action-failed`.

QuarantineItemAction.description:
What the spam filter did with the message (mailcow's `quarantine.action`):
- `rejected` (`reject`): the message was rejected and only kept in the quarantine;
- `junk` (`add header`): the message was delivered to the junk folder, with a copy kept here;
- `subjectRewritten` (`rewrite subject`): the message was delivered with a rewritten subject;
- `other`: any other stored value, given verbatim in `filterAction`.

MassMailRecipientFailureCode.description:
Why the delivery to a mass mail recipient failed, a stable code clients translate (gap G16), next
to Dovecot's reply in `failureReason`:

- `overQuota`: the mailbox is full (enhanced status `x.2.2`);
- `mailboxUnavailable`: Dovecot refused the mailbox, for example because it was deleted or
  deactivated after the job was created (enhanced status `5.1.x`, or a `550` reply);
- `rejected`: any other permanent refusal;
- `temporaryFailurePersisted`: Dovecot refused temporarily on every attempt (the first try and 3
  retries within 5 minutes);
- `connectionFailed`: Dovecot's local delivery could not be reached or did not answer within the
  delivery deadline on every attempt;
- `interrupted`: this API stopped while the delivery was in progress; it is never retried, so that
  no recipient receives two copies.

Clients show unknown codes like `rejected`.

MailboxMailSettingsPatch.description:
JSON Merge Patch of the mail settings. Changing `taggedMailHandling` needs `delimiterAction`;
changing a TLS value needs `tlsPolicy`. A value sent equal to its current value is not a change.
At least one member is required.

MailboxConflict.description:
The request conflicts with the current state. Nothing was changed. Possible types:
- `/api/v2/problems/address-in-use`: the address is held by a mailbox, resource, alias or
  temporary alias (extensions `address`, `holder`);
- `/api/v2/problems/mailbox-limit-reached`: the domain's mailbox limit is reached (extensions
  `domain`, `count`, `limit`);
- `/api/v2/problems/mailbox-not-renamable`: only active and login-disabled mailboxes can be
  renamed;
- `/api/v2/problems/domain-alias-limit-reached`: keeping the old address as an alias needs room
  for one more alias (extensions `domain`, `count`, `limit`).
